true,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-27","committee":"Children, Young People and Education Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-02","committee":"Culture and Neighbourhoods Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Public Health and Health Integration Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Housing Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Economic Development, Transport and Climate Emergency Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-12","committee":"Adult Social Care Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Culture and Neighbourhoods Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Housing Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Children, Young People and Education Scrutiny Commission","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","time":"5:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Council","time":"5:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cabinet.leicester.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Investment income declined 30% year-on-year from \u00a36.4m to \u00a34.5m, driven by falling interest rates.","lead":"Declining investment returns reduce non-tax revenue available to the council and impact its overall financial position; this is a leading indicator of cash yield pressure likely to continue if rates remain low.","iso":"2026-07-08","paper_title":"Treasury Management Full Year review 2025 26 OSC","paper_url":"https://leicester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s172739/172739.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"The council's \u00a35m property fund investment has depreciated by \u00a30.7m to a current valuation of \u00a34.3m, showing market risk on strategic investments.","lead":"The council's exposure to property market risk and ability to absorb unrealised losses is relevant to its overall financial resilience; the decision to halt new property investments suggests either risk appetite reduction or capital constraints.","iso":"2026-07-08","paper_title":"Treasury Management Full Year review 2025 26 OSC","paper_url":"https://leicester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s172739/172739.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Three loans made under the council's Investment Strategy have encountered repayment difficulties, totalling \u00a32.7m.","lead":"Lenders assessing the council need to understand its exposure to third-party borrowers and its track record on collecting strategic loans, particularly when borrowers fall behind on obligations.","iso":"2026-07-08","paper_title":"Treasury Management Full Year review 2025 26 OSC","paper_url":"https://leicester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s172739/172739.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-08","committee":"Overview Select Committee","paper_title":"Treasury Management Full Year review 2025 26 OSC","paper_url":"https://leicester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s172739/172739.pdf","headline":"Investment income declined 30% year-on-year from \u00a36.4m to \u00a34.5m, driven by falling interest rates.","detail":"The council's total investment income for 2025/26 was \u00a34.5m, slightly below the budget of \u00a34.7m. This represents a significant drop from \u00a36.4m in 2024/25, principally because interest rates fell during the year as the Bank of England cut base rate from 4.5% to 3.75%.","category":"income","matters":"Declining investment returns reduce non-tax revenue available to the council and impact its overall financial position; this is a leading indicator of cash yield pressure likely to continue if rates remain low.","figures":"2025/26 actual investment income \u00a34.5m (budget \u00a34.7m), compared to 2024/25 \u00a36.4m; represents 30% year-on-year decline","quote":"Total investment income during 2025/26 was \u00a34.5m. This is slightly lower than the \u00a34.7m budget, and considerably lower than the \u00a36.4m return in 2024/25 principally due to the gradual lowering of investment rates","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-08","committee":"Overview Select Committee","paper_title":"Treasury Management Full Year review 2025 26 OSC","paper_url":"https://leicester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s172739/172739.pdf","headline":"The council's \u00a35m property fund investment has depreciated by \u00a30.7m to a current valuation of \u00a34.3m, showing market risk on strategic investments.","detail":"The Threadneedle Property fund, purchased in 2018 for \u00a35m, has an unrealised loss of \u00a30.7m as at 31 March 2026. The fund returned \u00a3210k in dividends (4.2% on original capital) during 2025/26. The council states it does not expect to make new property investments in the near future.","category":"other","matters":"The council's exposure to property market risk and ability to absorb unrealised losses is relevant to its overall financial resilience; the decision to halt new property investments suggests either risk appetite reduction or capital constraints.","figures":"Original investment \u00a35m, valuation at 31/03/2026 \u00a34.3m, unrealised loss \u00a30.7m, dividend return \u00a3210k (4.2%)","quote":"The funds are held with the Threadneedle Property fund, with a valuation of \u00a34.3m which is also \u00a30.7m lower than the original purchase price of \u00a35m.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-08","committee":"Overview Select Committee","paper_title":"Treasury Management Full Year review 2025 26 OSC","paper_url":"https://leicester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s172739/172739.pdf","headline":"Three loans made under the council's Investment Strategy have encountered repayment difficulties, totalling \u00a32.7m.","detail":"Ethically Sourced Products Ltd's \u00a30.9m loan was due for repayment in 2025/26 and is overdue (working to resolve by early 2026/27). Leicester Community Sports Arena Ltd is currently in arrears on a \u00a31.4m loan. Leicester Hockey Club's \u00a30.4m loan fell into arrears but has now returned to repayment schedule. These represent credit risk on loans made for strategic return purposes.","category":"income","matters":"Lenders assessing the council need to understand its exposure to third-party borrowers and its track record on collecting strategic loans, particularly when borrowers fall behind on obligations.","figures":"Ethically Sourced Products Ltd \u00a30.9m (overdue), Leicester Community Sports Arena Ltd \u00a31.4m (in arrears), Leicester Hockey Club \u00a30.4m (previously in arrears, recovered)","quote":"Ethically Sourced Products Ltd loan was due for repayment in 2025/26, we are currently working with them to resolve by early 2026/27. Leicester Community Sports Arena Ltd is currently in arrears in relation to the repayment of their loan.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-08","committee":"Overview Select Committee","paper_title":"Treasury Management Full Year review 2025 26 OSC","paper_url":"https://leicester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s172739/172739.pdf","headline":"The council is reversing its 2025/26 borrowing strategy in 2026/27, financing \u00a390m of capital from reserves instead of new debt to reduce borrowing costs.","detail":"The 2026/27 budget moves \u00a390m of previously approved capital expenditure back to reserve financing. This reversal is attributed to 'limited improvements in the financial position' during 2025/26, allowing the council to avoid continued external borrowing and reduce ongoing revenue costs of debt service.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The rapid reversal of strategy signals the underlying cash pressure was temporary or has eased; however, it also indicates reliance on reserves for capital spending rather than sustained operational improvement.","figures":"\u00a390 million of capital expenditure reverting from borrowing to reserve financing in 2026/27","quote":"However limited improvements in the financial position have allowed this to be reversed in the 2026/27 budget, reducing our borrowing costs going forward.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-08","committee":"Overview Select Committee","paper_title":"Treasury Management Full Year review 2025 26 OSC","paper_url":"https://leicester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s172739/172739.pdf","headline":"After 15 years of internal borrowing, Leicester took on \u00a3110m in new PWLB debt in 2025/26 to support capital spending and maintain minimum cash balances.","detail":"Without external borrowing, the council's cash would have fallen below the \u00a325m minimum threshold by end of 2024/25. They borrowed \u00a3110m from PWLB, raising gross debt from \u00a3197m to \u00a3242m, and maintained cash balances of \u00a362m. This marks a significant break from the council's historical reliance on internal cash reserves.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A lender needs to understand why a council abandoned 15 years of cash-funded capital spending and entered the debt markets; this signals underlying cash pressure and changing financial dynamics.","figures":"\u00a3110m new PWLB borrowing, gross debt increased from \u00a3197m (30/09/2025) to \u00a3242m (31/03/2026), closing cash balances \u00a362m","quote":"During 2025/26 the Council increased its actual borrowing after many years of using internal borrowing as part of its capital financing. This new borrowing totalled \u00a3110m from the Public Loans Works Board (PWLB).","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-13"},"name":"Leicester City UA","findings_total":47,"news_drafts_total":3}}